Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>2  A list to choose from</H1>
<HR>
A list you can only look at isn't much use: an application needs a list you can <I>choose from</I>, the way you
choose files in a directory display. In this chapter you'll build the second toolkit module, <B>ListView</B>,
and use it to show the contacts in columns, with headings, that you can select with the mouse and move
through with the keyboard.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>What a list needs</H2>
Think about how a directory display behaves, and you have the list's plan. RISC OS applications all choose
things the same way, and the Style Guide spells it out:<P>
<UL>
<LI>Clicking Select on an item selects it, and deselects everything else.<P>
<LI>Clicking Adjust on an item selects it, or deselects it, leaving the others as they are. That's how you
choose several.<P>
<LI>Clicking Select in an empty part of the window deselects everything.<P>
<LI>Double-clicking an item opens it.<P>
<LI>Clicking Menu over an item that isn't selected selects it just while the menu is open (a
<I>temporary selection</I>), so the menu can act on it. Chapter 3 uses that.<P>
<LI>Selected items are shown in reverse: white on black.<P>
</UL>
And, like any window with a lot in it, the list should scroll, and the keyboard should work: the arrow keys
to move up and down, Page Up and Page Down, Home and Copy (End) for the first and last, Return to open.<P>
None of that is about contacts: it's how every list behaves. So it goes in the toolkit, where !Organiser can
use it too - for its to-do list, its search results, and more.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Using ListView</H2>
Here is !Contacts2's MainWindow. The window is made as before; the list takes over drawing it:<P>
<PRE>
export class MainWindow {
  constructor(task, book) {
    this.task = task;
    this.book = book;
    const w = this.win = task.createWindow({
      title: 'Contacts', x: 120, y: 70,
      w: 600, h: 380, extent: { w: 600, h: 380 },
      colours: { workBg: 0 }, workButton: 'clickdragdouble',
      flags: { back: true, close: true, title: true, toggle: true,
        vscroll: true, size: true, moveable: true },
    });
    this.list = new ListView(w, {
      key: (c) =&gt; c.id,
      columns: [
        { title: 'Name', width: 190, text: nameOf },
        { title: 'Phone', width: 130, text: phoneOf },
        { title: 'Email', width: 190, text: (c) =&gt; c.email },
        { title: 'Category', width: 90, text: (c) =&gt; c.category },
      ],
    });
    this.list.on('open', (c) =&gt; this.show(c));
    w.on('close', () =&gt; task.quit());
    w.open({ behind: 'top' });
    this.refresh();
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------ showing the book
  refresh() {
    this.list.setItems(this.book.list());
  }

  /** Show a contact's details (for now, in a message box). */
  show(c) {
    const lines = [nameOf(c), c.company, phoneOf(c), c.email];
    reportError(lines.filter(Boolean).join(', '),
      { appName: 'Contacts', category: 'info' });
  }
}
</PRE>
You give a ListView the window to fill and a description of the list:<P>
<DL>
<DT>columns
<DD>an array of columns, each with a heading (<CODE>title</CODE>), a width in pixels, and a function that
gives the text to show in it for an item. <CODE>nameOf</CODE> and <CODE>phoneOf</CODE> from the Model are
just such functions; the other two are arrow functions that pick out a field.<P>
<DT>key
<DD>a function that gives what tells one item from another - here each contact's id. More on this below.<P>
</DL>
Then <CODE>list.setItems(array)</CODE> tells it what to show (MainWindow's <CODE>refresh</CODE> gives it the
Book's sorted list), and <CODE>list.on('open', fn)</CODE> asks to be told when an item is opened. For now,
opening a contact just shows a little of it in a message box: chapter 3 opens a proper dialogue box instead.
The message box is the desktop's <CODE>reportError(message, options)</CODE>, the box your programs' mistakes
appeared in, in the first book. Its options change how it looks: <CODE>appName</CODE> puts <I>Message from
Contacts</I> in the title (without it the title is just <I>Error</I>), and <CODE>category: 'info'</CODE> shows
the "information" picture rather than the warning one. <CODE>lines.filter(Boolean)</CODE> keeps only the parts
that count as true - the ones that aren't empty or missing - because <CODE>Boolean(x)</CODE> is true or false as
<CODE>x</CODE> counts, so a contact without a company doesn't get an empty gap between two commas.<P>
Notice the window's <CODE>workButton: 'clickdragdouble'</CODE>: it asks the Wimp to report clicks, drags and
double-clicks in the work area, which a list needs.<P>
Run !Contacts2 and try the mouse and keyboard. Here Braben, Green and Ives have been chosen with Select and
