<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>How ListView works</H2>
ListView is about 180 lines, in <CODE>Toolkit.ListView</CODE>. It's an Emitter, so it can tell the program when
something happens. Here is how it starts:<P>
<PRE>
const ROW = 20;

export class ListView extends Emitter {
  constructor(win, { columns, key = (x) =&gt; x, top = 0 }) {
    super();
    Object.assign(this, { win, columns, key, top });
    this.items = [];
    this.sel = new Set();            // keys of the selected items
    this.current = null;             // key of the row the keys move
    this.temporary = false;          // selected just for a menu
    win.useCanvas((g, r) =&gt; this.draw(g, r));
    win.on('click', (ev) =&gt; this.click(ev));
    win.on('doubleclick', (ev) =&gt; this.double(ev));
    win.on('drag', (ev) =&gt; this.drag(ev));
    win.on('key', (ev) =&gt; this.key(ev));
    win.on('open', (ev) =&gt; { this.resize(ev.w); });
  }

  get width() {
    return this.columns.reduce((a, c) =&gt; a + c.width, 0);
  }
  rowY(i) { return this.top + ROW * (i + 1); }       // after headings
  indexAt(y) { return Math.floor((y - this.top) / ROW) - 1; }

  setItems(items) {
    this.items = items;
    const keys = new Set(items.map(this.key));
    for (const k of [...this.sel]) {
      if (!keys.has(k)) this.sel.delete(k);
    }
    this.resize();
    this.win.invalidate();
  }
  resize(w = this.win.w) {
    this.win.setExtent({
      w: Math.max(w, this.width + 8),
      h: Math.max(this.win.h, this.rowY(this.items.length) + 4),
    });
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>constructor(win, { columns, key = (x) =&gt; x, top = 0 })</CODE> takes the object you give it apart
straight away: <CODE>{ columns, key, top }</CODE> in the brackets makes three variables from the properties
of the same names. This is called <I>destructuring</I>. <CODE>key = (x) =&gt; x</CODE> gives a
<I>default</I>: if you don't give a key, each item is its own key. (<CODE>top</CODE>, the room to leave above
the list, is for a toolbar in chapter 5.)<P>
<CODE>Object.assign(this, { win, columns, key, top })</CODE> copies all four onto the ListView in one go -
the same as four lines of <CODE>this.win = win;</CODE> and so on.<P>
Then the ListView gives the window its redraw function and listens for its events. From here on, the window
is the ListView's to manage.<P>
<H3>Rows and positions</H3>
Every row is <CODE>ROW</CODE> (20) pixels high, and the headings take the first row. Two small methods convert
between rows and positions:<P>
<UL>
<LI><CODE>rowY(i)</CODE> gives the top of row <CODE>i</CODE> in the work area.<P>
<LI><CODE>indexAt(y)</CODE> goes the other way: which row is at height <CODE>y</CODE>? Dividing by the row
height and rounding down (<CODE>Math.floor</CODE>) does it. Finding out what was clicked from where is
called <I>hit-testing</I>.<P>
</UL>
<CODE>width</CODE> is a getter, as in chapter 1. It adds up the columns' widths with <CODE>reduce</CODE>, an
array method that goes through the array carrying a running total: <CODE>(a, c) =&gt; a + c.width</CODE> is
called with the total so far and each column in turn, starting from 0.<P>
<H3>The work area's size</H3>
<CODE>setItems</CODE> keeps the items, and then <CODE>resize</CODE> sets the window's <I>extent</I> - the size
of its work area - to fit them all, with <CODE>setExtent</CODE>. That's what makes the scroll bar work: with
ten contacts the extent is no taller than the window, but with a hundred it's much taller, and you can scroll
through them. (The window's <CODE>open</CODE> event calls <CODE>resize</CODE> too, so that making the window
wider makes the work area wider.)<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Drawing only what shows</H2>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ drawing
  draw(g, r) {
    g.font = '14px Homerton';
    g.textBaseline = 'middle';
    const first = Math.max(0, this.indexAt(r.y0));
    const last = Math.min(this.items.length - 1, this.indexAt(r.y1));
    for (let i = first; i &lt;= last; i++) {
      const x = this.items[i], y = this.rowY(i);
      const on = this.isSelected(x);
      g.fillStyle = wimpColour(on ? 7 : 0);    // selected: reversed
      g.fillRect(r.x0, y, r.x1 - r.x0, ROW);
      g.fillStyle = wimpColour(on ? 0 : 7);
      this.row(g, this.columns.map((c) =&gt; c.text(x)), y);
    }
    // the headings stay at the top of the visible area
    const hy = Math.max(this.top, r.y0 + this.top);
    g.fillStyle = wimpColour(1);
    g.fillRect(r.x0, hy, r.x1 - r.x0, ROW);
    g.fillStyle = wimpColour(7);
    g.font = 'bold 14px Homerton';
    this.row(g, this.columns.map((c) =&gt; c.title), hy);
  }
  /** Draw one row of texts, each clipped to its column. */
  row(g, texts, y) {
    let x = 6;
    texts.forEach((t, k) =&gt; {
      const w = this.columns[k].width;
      g.save();
      g.beginPath(); g.rect(x, y, w - 8, ROW); g.clip();
      g.fillText(String(t ?? ''), x, y + ROW / 2 + 1);
      g.restore();
      x += w;
    });
  }
</PRE>
A list may have thousands of rows, but only a screenful can be seen. The redraw function is given the
visible part of the work area as <CODE>r</CODE> (as in the first book), so <CODE>draw</CODE> works out the
first and last rows in it with <CODE>indexAt</CODE> and draws only those. That keeps the list quick however
long it grows.<P>
Each row is a background rectangle, then the columns' texts. For a selected row the colours are swapped, black
background and white text, as the Style Guide asks. <CODE>row</CODE> draws each text <I>clipped</I> to its
column (<CODE>g.clip()</CODE> after a rectangle limits drawing to that rectangle, until
<CODE>g.restore()</CODE>), so a long email address can't spill into the next column.<P>
The headings are drawn last, at the top of the <I>visible</I> area (<CODE>r.y0</CODE>) rather than the top of
the work area, so they stay put when you scroll and cover the rows beneath them.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>The selection</H2>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ the selection
  selected() {
    return this.items.filter((x) =&gt; this.sel.has(this.key(x)));
  }
  isSelected(x) { return this.sel.has(this.key(x)); }
  select(x, only = true) {
    if (only) this.sel.clear();
    this.sel.add(this.key(x));
    this.current = this.key(x);
    this.changed();
  }
  selectAll() {
    for (const x of this.items) this.sel.add(this.key(x));
    this.changed();
  }
  clear() { this.sel.clear(); this.changed(); }
  changed() {
    this.win.invalidate();
    this.emit('select', this.selected());
  }
  /** A menu opened over an unselected row: put things back. */
  endTemporary() {
    if (this.temporary) { this.temporary = false; this.clear(); }
  }
</PRE>
The selection is a Set (chapter 1) of the selected items' <I>keys</I>. This is why a ListView needs a
<CODE>key</CODE> function. The items it's given are replaced each time the list is refreshed - from chapter 3
on, the Book makes a new object for a contact whenever it's changed - so remembering the objects themselves
wouldn't work.
Their ids stay the same, so remembering the ids does. That's also why <CODE>setItems</CODE> removes the keys of
any items that have gone, and keeps the rest: the selection survives a refresh.<P>
<CODE>selected()</CODE> gives the selected items, in list order, by filtering the items for the ones whose
key is in the Set. Every change to the selection goes through <CODE>changed()</CODE>, which redraws the list and
emits <CODE>select</CODE> so the program can, for example, shade menu items that need a selection (chapter 4).<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>The mouse</H2>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ the mouse
  click(ev) {
    // (a click on the headings)
    if (ev.y &lt; this.win.scrollY + this.top + ROW) return true;
    const i = this.indexAt(ev.y), x = this.items[i];
    if (ev.button === 'menu') {
      this.temporary = !!x &amp;&amp; !this.isSelected(x);
      if (this.temporary) this.select(x);
      this.emit('menu', { ev, temporary: this.temporary });
      return true;
    }
    wimp.setCaret(this.win);          // the list takes the keys
    if (!x) { if (ev.button === 'select') this.clear(); return true; }
    if (ev.button === 'adjust') {
      const k = this.key(x);
      if (this.sel.has(k)) this.sel.delete(k); else this.sel.add(k);
      this.current = k;
      this.changed();
    } else this.select(x);
    return true;
  }
  double(ev) {
    const x = this.items[this.indexAt(ev.y)];
    if (x &amp;&amp; ev.button === 'select') this.emit('open', x);
    return true;
  }
  drag(ev) {
    const x = this.items[this.indexAt(ev.y)];
    if (!x || ev.button === 'menu') return true;
    if (!this.isSelected(x)) this.select(x);
    this.emit('drag', ev);
    return true;
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>click</CODE> follows the Style Guide's rules. First it ignores clicks on the headings. Then it finds the
row with <CODE>indexAt</CODE>, and the item there (<CODE>undefined</CODE> if the click was below the last
row):<P>
<UL>
<LI>Menu: if the item isn't selected, it's selected for now and <CODE>temporary</CODE> remembers that. Either
way, the program is told with a <CODE>menu</CODE> event, so it can open its menu. (<CODE>!!x</CODE> is two
"not"s: it turns any value into <CODE>true</CODE> or <CODE>false</CODE> as it counts, so
<CODE>temporary</CODE> is <CODE>false</CODE> rather than <CODE>undefined</CODE> when there's no item.)<P>
<LI>Otherwise the list takes the input focus with <CODE>wimp.setCaret(this.win)</CODE> (an invisible caret),
so that the keys come to it.<P>
<LI>Select below the last row clears the selection.<P>
<LI>Adjust adds the item to the selection, or takes it out if it was already there.<P>
<LI>Select selects just this item.<P>
</UL>
Returning <CODE>true</CODE> tells the Wimp the click has been dealt with. <CODE>double</CODE> emits
<CODE>open</CODE> for a double-click with Select, and <CODE>drag</CODE> tells the program a drag has started
on the selection (chapter 8 uses it to drag contacts out of the window).<P>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>The keyboard</H2>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ the keyboard
  key(ev) {
    const n = this.items.length;
    if (!n) return undefined;
    const page = Math.max(1, Math.floor(this.win.h / ROW) - 2);
    let i = this.items.findIndex((x) =&gt; this.key(x) === this.current);
    switch (ev.code) {
      case 0x18F: i = Math.max(0, i - 1); break;          // up
      case 0x18E: i = Math.min(n - 1, i + 1); break;      // down
      case 30: i = 0; break;                              // Home
      case 0x18B: i = n - 1; break;                       // Copy/End
      case 0x19F: i = Math.max(0, i - page); break;       // Page Up
      case 0x19E: i = Math.min(n - 1, i + page); break;   // Page Down
      case 13:
        if (i &gt;= 0) this.emit('open', this.items[i]);
        return true;
      default: return undefined;                          // not ours
    }
    this.select(this.items[i]);
    this.ensureVisible(this.items[i]);
    return true;
  }
  ensureVisible(x) {
    const y = this.rowY(this.items.indexOf(x)), w = this.win;
    if (y &lt; w.scrollY + this.top + ROW) {
      w.scrollTo(w.scrollX, Math.max(0, y - this.top - ROW));
    } else if (y + ROW &gt; w.scrollY + w.h) {
      w.scrollTo(w.scrollX, y + ROW - w.h);
    }
  }
</PRE>
The keys are handled with <CODE>switch</CODE>, a neater way of writing a row of <CODE>if</CODE>s that all
compare one value: JavaScript compares <CODE>ev.code</CODE> with each <CODE>case</CODE> in turn and runs the code
after the first that matches, up to a <CODE>break</CODE> (which jumps out of the <CODE>switch</CODE>) or a
<CODE>return</CODE>; <CODE>default</CODE> catches everything else. A <CODE>case</CODE> without a
<CODE>break</CODE> would run on into the next one, so each ends with one.<P>
The keys move the <I>current</I> row: the one last clicked or moved to. <CODE>findIndex</CODE> finds its
position in the list (or -1 if there isn't one). Each key works out a new position - one up, one down, a page
up or down, the first, the last - clamped with <CODE>Math.max</CODE> and <CODE>Math.min</CODE> so it can't go
off either end. Then the new row is selected and <CODE>ensureVisible</CODE> scrolls, if it has to, so it can be
seen. Return opens the current row.<P>
For any other key, <CODE>key</CODE> returns <CODE>undefined</CODE>: nothing. As in the first book, that lets the
key go on to the rest of the desktop, so F12 and the other keys still work.<P>
<CODE>ensureVisible</CODE> compares the row's position with the part of the work area showing
(<CODE>scrollY</CODE> to <CODE>scrollY + h</CODE>, less the headings) and scrolls just far enough, with
<CODE>scrollTo</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Every RISC OS list chooses the same way: Select selects one, Adjust adds or removes one, Select on
nothing clears, double-click opens, Menu can select temporarily, the selection is shown in reverse.<P>
<LI>ListView (the toolkit) draws a list of items in columns on a window, handles the mouse and keyboard, and
emits <CODE>select</CODE>, <CODE>open</CODE>, <CODE>menu</CODE> and <CODE>drag</CODE>.<P>
<LI>Hit-testing: work out what's under the pointer from where it is (<CODE>indexAt</CODE>).<P>
<LI>Draw only the rows in the visible area; set the extent to fit all of them so the window scrolls.<P>
<LI>Remember a selection by the items' keys, not the items, so it survives a refresh.<P>
<LI>Destructuring (<CODE>{ columns, key }</CODE> in a parameter list) picks properties out of an object;
<CODE>= value</CODE> gives a default; <CODE>Object.assign</CODE> copies properties; <CODE>reduce</CODE> works
through an array keeping a total; <CODE>findIndex</CODE> finds a position.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Show how many contacts are selected in the window's title (<I>Contacts: 3 selected</I>), using the
list's <CODE>select</CODE> event.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Add a Company column between Name and Phone.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Make Ctrl-A select every contact. (Its key code is 1. Listen for the window's <CODE>key</CODE>
event in MainWindow, after the ListView has been made.)<P>
<DT><DD>4  Why does ListView's <CODE>key</CODE> method return <CODE>undefined</CODE> for keys it doesn't use,
rather than <CODE>false</CODE>?<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  In MainWindow's constructor:
<PRE>
this.list.on('select', (items) =&gt; {
  if (items.length) w.setTitle(`Contacts: ${items.length} selected`);
  else w.setTitle('Contacts');
});
</PRE>
<DT><DD>2  Add a column to the array, and make the window a little wider:
<PRE>
{ title: 'Company', width: 140, text: (c) =&gt; c.company },
</PRE>
<DT><DD>3  In MainWindow's constructor, after the ListView:
<PRE>
w.on('key', (ev) =&gt; {
  if (ev.code === 1) { this.list.selectAll(); return true; }
});
</PRE>
<DT><DD>4  Returning <CODE>false</CODE> would tell the Wimp the key had been dealt with, so it would stop
there. Returning nothing lets it go on to the rest of the desktop (as chapter 8 of the first book explained),
so keys such as F12 keep working.<P>
</DL>

